BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pOjVhAQ46w7OevWE-NG7z
SUMMARY:Advancing Genetic Code Expansion: From Genome-Wide Host Reprogrammi
	ng to Innovative Biological Applications
DTSTAMP:20260805T111503Z
DTSTART:20260806T180000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Mengxi Zhang  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defe
	nse  Department: Chemistry  Location: BioScience Research Collaborative 28
	2  Genetic code expansion (GCE) enables noncanonical amino acids (ncAAs) w
	ith diverse chemical and physical properties to be introduced into protein
	s at defined positions. This technology has provided valuable tools for pr
	otein labeling\, functional regulation\, biomolecular engineering and the 
	investigation of biological processes. Despite the rapid development of GC
	E\, many genetically encoded ncAAs have been demonstrated primarily at the
	 level of protein incorporation\, while their potential for solving broade
	r biological and engineering problems remains underexplored. In addition\,
	 the production of ncAA-containing proteins is often limited by inefficien
	t stop-codon suppression and by the cellular environment in which the orth
	ogonal translation system operates. In this dissertation\, we expand the a
	pplications of genetically encoded ncAAs in material engineering and live-
	cell imaging and investigate endogenous host factors that regulate ncAA-co
	ntaining protein production.  To introduce material-binding properties int
	o proteins and living cells\, 3\,4-dihydroxy-L-phenylalanine (DOPA)\, a ca
	techol-containing amino acid found in mussel adhesive proteins\, was genet
	ically incorporated into proteins at defined positions. Optimization of th
	e DOPA incorporation system enabled the production of homogeneous DOPA-con
	taining proteins with enhanced binding to a broad range of organic and ino
	rganic materials. The material-binding capability of DOPA was further comb
	ined with intrinsically disordered proteins to generate adhesive antifouli
	ng proteins. Extending this strategy from proteins to living cells\, DOPA 
	was incorporated into a bacterial surface protein to substantially enhance
	 bacterial attachment to different materials. The improved interaction bet
	ween engineered bacteria and polyethylene terephthalate (PET) also facilit
	ated bacterial degradation of PET\, demonstrating the application of GCE i
	n protein\, material and cellular engineering.  In addition to creating ne
	w protein functions\, genetically encoded ncAAs can serve as compact probe
	s for studying dynamic biological processes. A series of rotor-based fluor
	escent ncAAs was developed to monitor local protein microenvironment chang
	es in living cells. Among these amino acids\, AnapTh displayed strong resp
	onses to environmental polarity and viscosity. Computational redesign of a
	n aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase enabled the site-specific incorporation of Ana
	pTh into proteins in mammalian cells. Placement of AnapTh at selected resi
	dues allowed real-time monitoring of local environmental changes during th
	e aggregation of human superoxide dismutase and Huntingtin proteins and du
	ring the clustering of STIM1 and CRY2. Distinct fluorescence responses obs
	erved at different incorporation sites revealed spatially resolved changes
	 within individual protein regions\, providing a method to visualize prote
	in aggregation\, clustering and their reverse processes with high spatial 
	precision.  While these studies demonstrate the functional potential of ge
	netically encoded ncAAs\, their broader utility depends on efficient and f
	aithful ncAA-containing protein production. To investigate how the host ce
	ll contributes to this process\, a genome-wide screen of the Escherichia c
	oli Keio single-gene knockout collection was performed using a dual-fluore
	scent amber-suppression reporter. Nine gene deletions reproducibly altered
	 reporter production across different ncAA incorporation systems\, indicat
	ing that GCE is regulated by diverse endogenous cellular processes. Among 
	these candidates\, rplI\, which encodes the 50S ribosomal protein bL9\, wa
	s further investigated. Deletion of rplI reduced overall protein expressio
	n\, increased relative amber-codon readthrough and decreased the translati
	on elongation rate\, linking ribosome dynamics to ncAA incorporation. Sequ
	ence Display was subsequently used to examine the sequence-function landsc
	ape of RplI and identified its C-terminal region as an informative target 
	for further engineering.  Together\, this dissertation develops and explor
	es important applications of genetically encoded ncAAs while providing ins
	ights into the host translational mechanisms that govern their incorporati
	on.  &#160\;  (Department : Chemistry)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/event/43
	5451-advancing-genetic-code-expansion-from-genome-wide-hos\n\nFrom Rice Un
	iversity - Open to the Public
